# Layer 2 DHCP Relay Topology

## Reference Architecture | Layer 2 DHCP Relay with ROAS

### Conceptual Blueprint for Enterprise Control-Plane Isolation
  
## 🎯 Lab Objective

This repository contains a Cisco networking lab built to simulate an enterprise-grade environment focusing on Layer 2 switching separation,
Router-on-a-Stick (ROAS) sub-interface routing, multi-subnet DHCP Relay topology, and an isolated Out-of-Band (OOB) Management framework.

## 🗺️ Topology Overview

The lab leverages a multi-segment addressing design that explicitly strips Layer 3 capabilities away from the core distribution layer, preserving a pure Layer 2 switching architecture:
* **R1-DHCP_Server:** Houses central IP pools, handles global network exit routing, and anchors the upstream OOB gateway interface.
* **R2-DHCP_Relay:** Acts as the local default gateway (/22, /23, and /24 sub-interfaces) and converts client DHCP broadcasts into unicast relay tracking payloads.
* **L2-Switch:** Serves as a transparent Layer 2 transit node processing user VLANs (10, 20, 30) and a dedicated management plane (VLAN 99).
* **Endpoints:** Isolated user clusters alongside a mock Lubuntu administration server running inside production segments.

